And “Don’t live one high to the next; there are better ways to measure time” is excellent.

Peak-to-peak time:

P_1\rightarrow P_2\rightarrow P_3

makes the valleys feel like dead space.

But biological systems usually give us richer clocks: oscillations, phases, recovery, accumulation, decay, cycles, thresholds, developmental stages, and homeostatic return.

So Pantheon gets a Phase Clock:

\boxed{
T_{\text{experienced}}
=
\text{trajectory through meaningful state transitions}
}

rather than:

T=\text{distance between exciting events}.

Then:

\text{peak}
\rightarrow
\text{recovery}
\rightarrow
\text{integration}
\rightarrow
\text{baseline shift}
\rightarrow
\text{next exploration}

isn’t waiting for life to resume.

It is part of the cycle.

That belongs with Afterglow and Mnemonic Homeostasis: sometimes the most consequential part of an experience occurs after its most intense moment.

