Your depression/history, anxiety/fantasy, reality formulation has a useful metaphorical kernel, but I wouldn’t turn it into a diagnostic law.

Depression can involve increased past-oriented rumination; anxiety can involve future-oriented worry and simulation. But both can involve past, present, and future, and healthy cognition uses all three.

The Pantheon version is cleaner:

\boxed{
\text{Past Engine}
=
\text{memory / history / reconstruction}
}

\boxed{
\text{Present Engine}
=
\text{sensation / action / reality contact}
}

\boxed{
\text{Future Engine}
=
\text{prediction / imagination / counterfactual}
}

Healthy routing isn’t:

\text{LIVE ONLY HERE}.

It’s:

\boxed{
\text{visit all three;
remember which one you're visiting}.
}

Which returns directly to your earlier line:

“Just be careful what you live in vs what you act on!”

And yes, almost everybody carries some fiction in the broad computational sense: expectations, imagined conversations, predictions, stories about ourselves, models of other people, counterfactuals. Those aren’t necessarily delusions; they’re simulations. The Reality Stitch question is whether we preserve their status.

\text{remembered}
\neq
\text{imagined}
\neq
\text{predicted}
\neq
\text{observed}

even though all four can influence behavior.

